**FULL\-TIME JOB VACANCY**  

**Deputy Director, Economic, Social and Cultural Rights**  

**Environment, and Economic and Social Rights Division**  

**Multiple Locations Considered**  

**Application Deadline: October 6, 2026**



Human Rights Watch (HRW) is seeking a Deputy Director to lead its work on Economic, Social and Cultural (ESC) Rights with a focus on Health, Social Security, and Labor within a newly created Environment, and Economic and Social Rights Division. Candidates with deep expertise on public health, health and human rights, and health harms experienced by communities affected by fossil fuel pollution, are encouraged to apply. The Deputy will work as part of the division's management team and manage the ESC team responsible for this work, help develop and implement its strategy, coordinate with colleagues across HRW, uphold high standards of research and advocacy, and support fundraising for the division.



The successful candidate will be an effective leader, experienced in managing rigorous and complex research and maintaining organizational standards, and a compelling communicator with experience on key issues related to the position. They will have demonstrated experience developing and implementing strategic human rights interventions and understand how to bring innovate approaches to research, advocacy and communications to effect change.


**Position Overview**



The Deputy will be working with the Director and a new management team to supervise staff with an emphasis on diversity, equity and inclusion, managing stress and resilience of staff, and encouraging a healthy work/life balance while supervising and helping to develop areas of programmatic work consistent with HRW's strategy and the mandate of the new division. This full\-time position will be based in one of HRW's global offices, will work from the office on an as\-needed basis, and will report to the Director, based in Washington, D.C.


**Responsibilities:**



1\. Design and implement with the Director, divisional management team, and staff, a multi\-year strategy for the ESC team to advance research and advocacy consistent with the organization's Global Strategy and the mandate of the division;



2\. Help integrate members of the new division and provide day\-to\-day management and strategic leadership of the ESC team to develop, implement, and oversee strategies to advance this key area of work; help the team keep abreast of current research and discourse on these issues;



3\. Manage research ensuring sound project selection, rigorous investigative research methodologies, and compliance with organizational standards and mandate;



4\. Coordinate with other parts of the organization to strategically guide and advance work on these areas of work through support for research, helping to develop innovative communications and advocacy products, and developing new policies to support this work;



5\. Act as a senior representative and advocate for the work of the division internally;



6\. Act as a senior spokesperson and liaise with external actors to advocate on the work of the division;



7\. Represent HRW and the work of the division externally;



8\. Foster meaningful and equitable partnerships when working with local organizations and other movement partners;



9\. Work with HRW's development staff as needed to support HRW's fundraising efforts;



10\. Work with colleagues in advocacy and communications to develop multidisciplinary strategies to support the work to achieve impact;



11\. Identify and manage legal and security risks, together with the Director, security, program and other colleagues as appropriate in line with organizational policies;



12\. Travel domestically and internationally, as required for internal meetings, research missions, and advocacy opportunities; and



13\. Undertake other responsibilities as required.


**Management Responsibilities:**  

1\. Manage and guide staff in conjunction with the Director to deliver timely, high\-quality work that supports EESR's strategy and priorities;



2\. Lead, motivate and mentor staff by setting clear expectations, providing regular and constructive feedback, supporting balanced workloads and professional growth, and monitoring staff well\-being;



3\. Promote and inclusive, creative and positive work environment by soliciting diverse perspectives, modeling inclusive behavior and adhering to HRW's values;



4\. Provide appropriate and timely feedback on staff performance and guidance, coaching and training, as necessary;



5\. Contribute to putting in place systems, processes, and policies to ensure the efficient functioning of the team and its ability to manage projects and budgets and deliver timely outputs;


**Candidate Profile**



The successful candidate will be a thoughtful, collaborative, and strategic leader with deep knowledge and experience working on human rights issues related to health, labor, social security and the environment. They will have experience of conducting on\-the\-ground investigative human rights research, as well as managing researchers. They will be a persuasive advocate with a strong track record of driving change, building equitable partnerships, and shaping HRW's contribution to the global human rights movement.



They will be an experienced manager who communicates effectively across teams, builds consensus around key challenges and new research and advocacy approaches, comfortable managing experienced staff, and upholds the highest research standards in a culture of learning, trust, teamwork, and collaboration. They will be someone who operates from a place of principle and inclusive leadership.



They will champion health, labor and social security work throughout the organization, clearly explain technical concepts in plain language, and communicate the human rights dimensions of this work to internal and external audiences.


**Qualifications**


**Education:** A graduat

**The Position**
Reporting to the President/CEO and serving as a member of BGCD's executive leadership team, the Chief Advancement Officer (CAO) is responsible for setting and leading a comprehensive advancement and marketing strategy for the organization. BGCD is seeking a seasoned, well\-connected fundraising executive to shape the future of an expanded advancement program — someone with deep, established relationships within Dallas' philanthropic, corporate, and civic community who can identify, cultivate, and secure new investors and strategic partners committed to the mission.
The Chief Advancement Officer will have organization\-wide responsibility for all channels of fundraising — annual fund, major gifts, planned giving, corporate and foundation relations, grants, and events — as well as marketing, communications, and public relations for the organization. S/he will also serve as a key advisor to the President/CEO and Board of Directors on philanthropic strategy, and will be responsible for donor stewardship, development operations, and driving new philanthropic investment to fuel BGCD's growth.
**Responsibilities**
**Executive Leadership**
* Serve as a key member of BGCD's executive leadership team, contributing to organization\-wide strategic planning and decision\-making;
* Act as a strategic advisor to the President/CEO and Board of Directors on philanthropic trends, donor sentiment, and advancement strategy; and
* Represent BGCD's advancement and marketing function externally as a senior spokesperson within the Dallas philanthropic and civic community.

**Fundraising, Growth \& Donor Stewardship**
* Drive new growth for BGCD by proactively identifying, cultivating, and securing new individual, corporate, and foundation investors — leveraging existing relationships within Dallas' philanthropic community to expand and diversify the donor base;
* Manage all fundraising activities, including designing and implementing strategies for generating increased revenue for programs and capital needs;
* Establish development goals and objectives, and determine the priority activities to be undertaken;
* Generate tours of the facilities and meetings with current and potential donors;
* Oversee organizational events;
* Create and nurture long\-term relationships with current and new funders;
* Customize communications and actions for major donors;
* Leverage the CEO and Board for meetings, calls, letters, etc. and provide preparatory materials such as bios, history, target ask, context of relationship, etc.;
* Personally participate in top\-level fundraising activities, including cultivation and solicitations; and
* Ensure stewardship plan is followed where all donors are feeling engaged and appreciated, and ensure funders are well stewarded.

**Board Management**
* Engage Board members with mutual respect as partners;
* Lead Board Development Committee (set agenda, lead meetings, distribute materials for meetings, manage Committee members' relationships, plan and implement fundraising activities); and
* Develop fundraising reports for the Board and make presentations at Board meetings.

**Planning and Analysis**
* Manage prospect lists;
* Lead bookings forecasting, ensuring extemporaneous updates, as well as highlighting known changes; and
* Track outcomes and impact and analyze results.

**Financial Management, Human Resources \& Team Leadership**
* Manage the Development department's annual operating budget and revenue plan;
* Participate, as required, in the annual financial audit;
* Provide direct supervision, strategic direction, coaching, and performance management to the development and marketing team.
* Set individual and team fundraising goals aligned with organizational growth targets, and hold direct reports accountable for results;
* Ensure that sound human resource practices are in place; and
* Maintain an environment that attracts, retains, and motivates a diverse team of top\-quality development and marketing staff.

**Development Operations**
* Maintain database ensuring the accuracy and timeliness of information (capture and create action items, all major interactions, personal information, relationships);
* Partner with Finance to enhance and maintain gift processing policies and procedures, as well as to ensure thorough and accurate record\-keeping; and
* Establish and maintain appropriate Development policies and procedures.

**Marketing \& Communications**
* Provide leadership and strategic direction to the Marketing and Communications function, ensuring brand consistency and mission\-aligned messaging across all channels;
* Ensure marketing and communications efforts support donor cultivation, stewardship, and new investor engagement; and
* Oversee public relations efforts that elevate BGCD's visibility and reputation within the Dallas community.

**Candidate Qualifications / Experience**
Experience: Minimum eight to ten years of nonprofit development and marketing experience, including prior senior leadership or executive\-level experience. Managing a team of at least three full\-time employees, with demonstrated success building and scaling a development function. Experience collaborating with a volunteer Board at the executive level. Deep, established network within Dallas' philanthropic, corporate, and civic community, with a demonstrated ability to translate relationships into new philanthropic investment. Must be able to clear a background check and history indicating fitness to work with children.
Education: A Bachelor's degree is required, and training that would provide the knowledge, skills, and abilities required for the successful performance of the essential job duties. Advanced degree preferred.
Certifications: CFRE strongly preferred.
**Competencies / Attributes**
The successful professional will have or be:
* Passionate about Boys \& Girls Clubs of Greater Dallas' vision and mission; personally committed to helping at\-risk youth graduate from high sc

